// Alarm -- turn a condition into at most one message, and a way to stop asking.
//
// The hard part of an alarm is never noticing the fault. It is the three ways
// of getting it wrong afterwards: firing on a glitch, firing again every cycle
// for a week, or firing once into a lost message and never again. This module
// answers all three and knows nothing about the transport -- wire `Send` to an
// SMS, a CAN frame or a println.
//
//   #Alarm frost  OnDelay=30000 OffDelay=60000 Repeat=3600000
//   frost.In  = Temp < FrostLimit
//   frost.Ack = Replied
//   println("FROST ", Temp) ? frost.Send
//   println("frost over")   ? frost.Clear
//
// OnDelay / OffDelay -- the condition must HOLD for OnDelay before it counts,
//   and must be gone for OffDelay before the alarm releases. A thermometer
//   sitting on the threshold crosses it hundreds of times an hour; without both
//   delays that is hundreds of messages. Make OffDelay the longer of the two:
//   it costs nothing to release late, and it is what stops the alarm flapping.
//
// Repeat -- how often to say it again while nobody has acknowledged. This is
//   the only thing bounding how much work the transport is asked to do, so it
//   is the number to raise when the bill arrives. Sending once and hoping is
//   not an option over a radio link; repeating without a limit is not either.
//
// Ack -- the receiver said it heard. That stops the repeats but does NOT clear
//   the alarm, because whether the fault is over is a question about the world,
//   not about the operator. A LATER episode still sends even if Ack is still
//   standing: a new fault is news regardless of what was acknowledged about the
//   last one.
//
// Send and Clear are one-cycle requests, in the same style as `.tx` -- they go
// high for exactly the cycle after the decision and clear themselves.
//
// ALL TIMES ARE IN MILLISECONDS and are ordinary variables, so they can be set
// per instance and patched in the field. They are counted on Tk, which sets the
// resolution: every deadline can be up to one tick late. Raise Tk.period on a
// slow board -- an alarm that answers within a second of a half-minute delay is
// still an alarm.
//
// STILL OPEN: none of this survives a reboot. A device that power-cycles during
// a fault forgets that it has already sent, and says it again. The plan is to
// persist the repeat LEVEL rather than a timestamp, so that no clock has to
// survive with it -- but the EEPROM side of that is not built yet.
//
#module Alarm
#timer    Tk 100 = 1                   // resolution in ms -- also the tick
#variable In:1     unsigned in  = 0    // the raw condition
#variable Ack:1    unsigned in  = 0    // the receiver acknowledged
#variable Active:1 unsigned out = 0    // the condition is real, not a glitch
#variable Send:1   unsigned out = 0    // one cycle: transmit the alarm now
#variable Clear:1  unsigned out = 0    // one cycle: transmit the all-clear
#variable OnDelay              = 1000  // ms the condition must hold to count
#variable OffDelay             = 1000  // ms it must be gone to release
#variable Repeat               = 60000 // ms between re-sends while unacknowledged
#variable Hold                 = 0     // ms In has kept its present value
#variable Age                  = 0     // ms since the last transmission
#variable Was:1    unsigned    = 0     // In as of its last change
#variable Sent:1   unsigned    = 0     // this episode has been announced

// Clear the one-shots first so any rule below still wins its own cycle.
Send  = 0 ? Send
Clear = 0 ? Clear

Tk = 1 ? timeout(Tk)

// How long the raw condition has held still. Both counters stop at the largest
// value that still means something, so a fault left standing for a month cannot
// walk its way to integer overflow and look fresh again.
Hold = Hold + Tk.period ? timeout(Tk)
Hold = OnDelay + OffDelay ? timeout(Tk) && Hold >= OnDelay + OffDelay
Hold = 0, Was = In ? In != Was

// The verdict. A condition that keeps flickering resets Hold on every flicker
// and so never reaches either delay -- which is exactly the point.
//
// `In == Was` is what makes that true. On the cycle In moves, the reset above
// has not been committed yet, so Hold still holds the OLD state's age -- which
// is almost always past the delay. Judging on it would fire on the transition
// itself and both delays would be dead code. Waiting for Was to catch up costs
// one cycle and is the whole guarantee.
Active = 1 ?  In && In == Was && !Active && Hold >= OnDelay
Active = 0 ? !In && In == Was &&  Active && Hold >= OffDelay

// Time since we last asked the transport to do something.
Age = Age + Tk.period ? timeout(Tk)
Age = Repeat ? timeout(Tk) && Age >= Repeat

// First announcement of an episode, and the repeat clock starts with it.
Send = 1, Sent = 1, Age = 0 ? Active && !Sent

// Say it again until someone admits hearing it.
Send = 1, Age = 0 ? Active && !Ack && Age >= Repeat

// All clear, once, on the way down -- and rearm for the next episode. Only if
// we announced this one: an alarm nobody was told about needs no retraction.
Clear = 1 ? !Active && Sent
Sent  = 0 ? !Active
#end
